1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I'm Owen Wilson and I'm a Scoutmaster with troop number 868 in Peoria, Arizona.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast is sponsored by backers like me.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
The other day my Scouts asked me if I knew how to make a mosquito repellent

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
and I told them I thought that the mosquitoes did a pretty good job of that all on their own.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
You're gonna have to think about that.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
I'm not explaining it.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Hey, this is podcast number 350..

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
This is Clark Green looking in the mailbag, as we are want to do.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
We have heard from Mike Bailey, who's the committee chair of troop 9626 in Shoreview, Minnesota,

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Who said I wanted to make a quick comment related to this week's podcast topic of registered committee members on boards of reviews.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
So that was the last podcast, or maybe a couple podcasts ago.

14
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
We've always had boards of review facilitated by an experienced and trained Scouter.

15
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
That said, we've recently begun the practice of requesting that a new Scout parent sit on at least one board of review,

16
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
preferably with an older Scout in the first year their son is in Scouting.

17
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
We feel it helps that parent understand the program better, get a glimpse into the future.

18
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
It's also been a great initial entry into approaching parents about becoming registered volunteers themselves

19
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Initially. our efforts have been very successful and appreciated.

20
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
We don't feel we're affecting the safety or the effectiveness of the border review process

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:20,000
And it's been a great way to familiarize new parents with how this Boy Scout program operates, differently from Cub Scouts.

22
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Well, thanks for that, Mike.

23
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
I don't have an argument with a parent sitting in as an observer on a border review,

24
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:35,000
other than the fact that sometimes things can get discussed in a border review that maybe need to remain confidential,

25
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
especially in the period of time when the board is discussing the Scout after they've spoken with the Scout

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
and before they approve the advancement.

27
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
So there is some merit to observing the questioning portion of the border review,

28
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
but I think after that it's time for the parent to stop observing.

29
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Heard from John Patrick Hickey. you said thanks so much for all the great things you share to advance Scouting-

30
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Great and fun stuff.

31
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Thanks, John.

32
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Hey, if you're around on Tuesday mornings around 8 am Eastern time,

33
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
you can come over to scoutmastercgcom and join us for a live chat.

34
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
If you keep an eye on our Facebook feed and our Twitter feed,

35
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,000
you'll see that the live chat is announced most every Tuesday morning-

36
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
not every Tuesday morning, but just about every Tuesday morning-

37
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
And you can come along and sign in and join us.

38
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:23,000
We have great fun.

39
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
We talk about important things and things that aren't important at all.

40
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
In addition to the frequent fliers who show up on a regular basis for our live chat,

41
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
we welcome Fernando Harutner, who's an assistant scoutmaster, soon to be scoutmaster

42
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
in Troop 552 in Doral, Florida.

43
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Ted, who's the scoutmaster of Troop 45 in the Chicago area,

44
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
And Robert, who's a council commissioner in Southwest Florida.

45
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
They all joined us for their first time on the chat this past week.

46
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Come on along and say hello if you have a chance.

47
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Before I go any further, thank you to all the folks who are scoutmastercgcom backers,

48
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:07,000
including Greg Nash, who added his name to the list of backers since our last podcast.

49
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
I also want to thank all the folks who support us over at Patreon as subscribers,

50
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
And if you'd like to become a backer or join our Patreon subscribers,

51
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
just go to scoutmastercgcom.

52
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
You'll find links to follow at the top right of any page

53
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
And there will be links in this episode's podcast notes.

54
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Before we get started, I want to tell you I had a great visit with Wade and Bucky.

55
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
They reached the Pennsylvania, New Jersey border on their Appalachian Trail through Hike,

56
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
which is relatively close to where we live, and they were in town.

57
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
We had a great visit. I was able to record some of it

58
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and I will be editing it down for inclusion in a future podcast,

59
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
But they're doing great

60
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Well. for the remainder of the podcast, I've got a couple of email questions to answer,

61
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
so let's get started. shall we

62
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:32,000
E-mail? that is folks,

63
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

64
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Now, back in podcast 348,

65
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
we asked for input about how scouts work with parents

66
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
And I heard from Andrea Cooperman who said:

67
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
I've been a PAC committee chair for six years,

68
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
an assistant scout master or committee chair for the last two years for a troop,

69
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
And now I'm getting ready to be a crew advisor.

70
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
I've dealt with many parents and I find mostly they just need to be heard.

71
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Whatever their complaint or concern,

72
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
they just want to know someone is listening.

73
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Many times all it takes is an understanding ear.

74
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
I try to get to know most of our families and their situations.

75
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
I do understand the anxiety many parents feel

76
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
about making sure their scout is achieving,

77
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
But if I asked them to look back on their own childhood

78
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
and how they went about learning the skills they have now.

79
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:35,000
they usually stop and say oh, and relax a little bit

80
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Once again. mostly they just want to be heard.

81
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
When they can get a person to listen, they'll unload.

82
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
I've learned to be flexible and compassionate

83
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
and, above all, not to judge.

84
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
You never really know what a family may be going through at home.

85
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,000
However you go about your life,

86
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
it's not our place as scouts to judge.

87
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
We're there for our scouts to help make their dreams a reality

88
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
and hopefully have some fun in the process.

89
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
If you're not having fun, you're doing it wrong.

90
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Well, thanks for that input, Andrea.

91
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
I really appreciate the benefit of your experience

92
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,000
in dealing with that.

93
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Listening is such an important skill,

94
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:13,000
as is applying that kind of non-judgmental compassion.

95
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
It can be tough.

96
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
It can be really, really tough,

97
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
But hey, you wouldn't be a scouter if you weren't tough, right?

98
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,000
I heard from Scott Williams,

99
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:28,000
who is with Scout Troop 534 in Flowery Branch, Georgia.

100
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
He said: thanks for all you do for scouting.

101
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
The question I have is:

102
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
how do you handle transportation to scouting events?

103
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
I have parents that sign up to provide us rides on campouts

104
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
and consistently have them cancel out at the last minute

105
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
and then have to scramble to get the other parents

106
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
to drive scouts to and from events.

107
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:54,000
Well, Scott, what you're telling me sounds completely normal.

108
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,000
I mean, I hear this sort of thing a lot

109
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
and I've experienced myself.

110
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
It's probably one of those things that is a feature

111
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,000
rather than a flaw that can be fixed.

112
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
Sometimes plans work and sometimes they don't.

113
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,000
I know that this is a very unsatisfactory answer,

114
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
but does it help at all?

115
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
And Scott got back to me and said: yes,

116
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
thanks for your reply.

117
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,000
It helped somewhat.

118
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
I have a sister troop that basically locks everything in

119
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
two weeks out and if you cancel

120
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
you're still charged for the trip.

121
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
and if there's an emergency that requires you to cancel,

122
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
I take it to heart to run my troop,

123
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
like you talk about on your podcast.

124
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
so I'm conflicted on trying this troop's method.

125
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,000
My reply to Scott was: you know,

126
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:39,000
either approach is going to require

127
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
approximately the same amount of energy and frustration.

128
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
I think at least one approach is positive

129
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,000
and inclusive and one is prohibitive and exclusive.

130
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:52,000
You can't force people to be considerate, responsible

131
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:53,000
and helpful.

132
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
You can only force them to be compliant,

133
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,000
which I'm not saying is necessarily a bad thing.

134
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
but trying to force people to be compliant

135
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
has its own set of frustrations

136
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
that is at least equal to, if not greater than,

137
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
the frustration of dealing with the vicissitudes

138
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
of families and human beings.

139
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
If your troop is like mine,

140
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
some families are consistently considerate,

141
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
responsible and helpful.

142
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Some are less. so

143
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
We tend to call on the ones

144
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
who are considerate, responsible and helpful

145
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
and not the ones who aren't.

146
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
And nothing about that is fair,

147
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
because some people will do more than others

148
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,000
and some people don't contribute at all.

149
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
And I can't really think honestly,

150
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
if you think about it right,

151
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
I can't think of any endeavor, anything.

152
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,000
You know, whether it's working with a community group,

153
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
whether it's working with scouts,

154
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
whether it's working with a church,

155
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
any kind of volunteer situation

156
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
where that isn't pretty much the way things go.

157
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
So kind of far-bibbed for me

158
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
to tell you how to handle this exactly.

159
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
There are different ways to handle it.

160
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
All's I'm saying is is that it can be equally frustrating

161
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
to handle it either way.

162
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
I would rather see us use as many positive

163
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
and inclusive methods as possible.

164
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
And Scott got back to me and said

165
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
once again: you have enlightened me.

166
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
You are the bodhisattva.

167
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
The bodhisattva-

168
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,000
Well, that's not a word you hear very often of scouting,

169
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:29,000
Thank you.

170
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,000
How about that?

171
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,000
The bodhisattva of scouting?

172
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Okay, all right, let's get back at it.

173
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
A person who sent me this last question

174
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
asked to remain anonymous and said this:

175
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
our cub pack charter is held by a community organization

176
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
but naturally run by our volunteers.

177
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Do we have to provide the community organization

178
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
with all the funds that we raise,

179
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
place the money in an account

180
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
under the community organization's tax identification number,

181
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
and do they dictate how we spend our money?

182
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
Ah well,

183
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
the short answer is yes.

184
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:20,000
The chartering organization owns all the assets

185
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:23,000
of a cub pack, a scout troop or a venturing crew.

186
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
I'm talking about BSA policy.

187
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
If you're outside of the BSA,

188
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
you need to look at the policies

189
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
of your scouting organization, of course,

190
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
But in the BSA, yes,

191
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:39,000
you must include the chartering organization's tax ID

192
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,000
on your bank accounts,

193
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
because the chartering organization

194
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
owns all of the bank accounts.

195
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
You have to remember that the chartering organization

196
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
is responsible to make sure that a cub pack,

197
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:53,000
a scout troop, a venture cruise finances

198
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
are administered properly.

199
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
They own the unit.

200
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
It's their job to make sure

201
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
that everything is handled responsibly.

202
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Now, that doesn't necessarily mean

203
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
that they're going to dictate exactly

204
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
how every penny is spent,

205
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
but they should have proper oversight

206
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,000
of all the finances of the unit that they charter.

207
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
In practice, most charter organizations

208
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
don't get very involved in the management of finances

209
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
or the program that the unit presents

210
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
because they entrust those responsibilities

211
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
and decisions to the volunteers involved.

212
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
A lot of times people volunteer

213
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
for a cub pack or a scout troop or a venture crew

214
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
and they have an incomplete understanding

215
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
of the way this whole chartering thing works.

216
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
but someone is registered

217
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
as the chartered organization representative

218
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
and they handle the relationship

219
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
between the unit's volunteers

220
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
and the chartered organization.

221
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
Most of the time. you know,

222
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
the charter for a given cub pack

223
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
or scout troop or a venturing crew

224
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
could be years and years old

225
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
and the present volunteers

226
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
and the folks at the chartering organization

227
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
don't know how any of it is arranged

228
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
because they weren't around when it's set up

229
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
and they're not often called on

230
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
to deal with those arrangements.

231
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
So there's a great resource called

232
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
the Chartered Organization Representative Guidebook

233
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and I'll have a link to that in the podcast notes.

234
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
That explains the way that this relationship works

235
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
and when money gets in the picture.

236
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,000
and of course, your cubs and your volunteers

237
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
have done all the effort and raised all the money

238
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
and now the chartering organization

239
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:25,000
seems to want to control it.

240
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
Well, take a deep breath,

241
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
because this is how every cub pack,

242
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
scout troop and venturing crew in the country

243
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
is administered. You're going to be fine

244
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
and I'm happy to answer any questions

245
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
that may help you thread your way through this.

246
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
but I think if you'll read that

247
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
Chartered Organization Representative Guidebook,

248
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
that will be helpful

249
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and just understand that

250
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
this is the way the relationship is. Nothing has changed.

251
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
It just may be that the Chartered Organization

252
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
is discovering they need to be involved

253
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
in things that they didn't understand before,

254
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
So I hope that helps.

255
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
